# BUG-293 — a mutated `List(T)` parameter cannot be called across a module
# boundary. THIS FIXTURE PINS BROKEN BEHAVIOUR ON PURPOSE.
#
# It is registered `smoke_run_fail` and it PASSES TODAY by failing to compile:
#
#   error: expected type '*T', found 'T'
#
# The callee's own emit declares `out: *std.ArrayList(i64)` (it can see its own
# body and knows the param is mutated); this call site emits a bare `addTwo(xs)`,
# because the caller decides addr-of by inspecting the CALLEE'S BODY and the body
# lookup stops at the module boundary — while the parameter lookup does not
# (§27b gave `lookupFnParams` a cross-module fallback; `lookupFnBody` never got
# one). Two sides of one convention consulting different oracles.
#
# WHEN BUG-293 IS FIXED THIS FIXTURE WILL GO RED. That is the signal, not a
# regression: rewrite it as a `smoke_run` asserting `bug293-cross=2`, exactly as
# the sibling positive control already does for the same-module direction. Do NOT
# re-baseline around it.
#
# The paired control is bug293_samemod_container_test.zbr — same call, same
# module, must print 2.
use bug293_xmod_container_lib exposing addTwo

def main()
    var xs: List(int) = List()
    addTwo(xs)
    print("bug293-cross=${xs.len}")
